{"records":[{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7fgg5452pr","title":"The SARS-CoV-2 interactome","description":"SARS-CoV-2 is an RNA virus whose success as a pathogen relies on its abilities to repurpose host RNA-binding proteins (RBPs) and to evade antiviral RBPs. To uncover the SARS-CoV-2 RNA interactome, we here develop a robust ribonucleoprotein (RNP) capture protocol and identify 109 host factors that directly bind to SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Applying RNP capture on another coronavirus HCoV-OC43 revealed evolutionarily conserved interactions between coronaviral RNAs and host proteins. Transcriptome analyses and knockdown experiments delineated 17 antiviral RBPs including ZC3HAV1, TRIM25, PARP12, and SHFL and 8 proviral RBPs such as EIF3D and CSDE1 which are responsible for co-opting multiple steps of the mRNA life cycle. This also led to the identification of LARP1, a downstream target of the mTOR signaling pathway, as an antiviral host factor that interacts with the SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Overall, this study provides a comprehensive list of RBPs regulating coronaviral replication and opens new avenues for therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-07T07:11:04.995Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7fgg5452pr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7fgg5452pr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sungyul","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-3207-1199"},{"first_name":"Young-suk","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-0220-9951"},{"first_name":"Yeon","last_name":"Choi","orcid":"0000-0003-4983-6573"},{"first_name":"Aheyon","last_name":"Son","orcid":"0000-0001-7945-3851"},{"first_name":"Youngran","last_name":"Park","orcid":"0000-0002-8621-1399"},{"first_name":"Kyung-Min","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0002-1373-5407"},{"first_name":"Jeesoo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jong-Seo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-8909-8440"},{"first_name":"V. Narry","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-3213-4965"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Proteomics","RNA","Interactome","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7bd5bhmhmz","title":"Genome-scale identification of SARS-CoV-2 and pan-coronavirus host factor networks. Schneider et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic has claimed the lives of more than one million people worldwide. The causative agent, SARS-CoV-2, is a member of the Coronaviridae family, which are viruses that cause respiratory infections of varying severity. The cellular host factors and pathways co-opted by SARS- CoV-2 and other coronaviruses in the execution of their life cycles remain ill-defined. To develop an extensive compendium of host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses (HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-229E), we performed parallel genome-scale CRISPR knockout screens. These screens uncovered multiple host factors and pathways with pan- coronavirus and virus-specific functional roles, including major dependency on glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, SREBP signaling, BMP signaling, and glycosylphosphatidylinositol biosynthesis, as well as a requirement for several poorly characterized proteins. We identified an absolute requirement for the VTT-domain containing protein TMEM41B for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses. This human Coronaviridae host factor compendium represents a rich resource to develop new therapeutic strategies for acute COVID-19 and potential future coronavirus spillover events.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T09:15:53.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7bd5bhmhmz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7bd5bhmhmz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ygj2yfj9k5","title":"A study of structural characterization of SARS-CoV-2. Sun et al","description":"The supplementary table S1-S7 of our paper entitled \"In vivo structural characterization of the SARS-CoV-2 RNA genome identifies host proteins vulnerable to repurposed drugs\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-16T16:04:56.181Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ygj2yfj9k5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ygj2yfj9k5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"LEI","last_name":"SUN","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pan","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaohui","last_name":"Ju","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jian","last_name":"Rao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Structure","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8gj97c4kgv","title":"Genome-wide mapping of SARS-CoV-2 RNA structures identifies therapeutically-relevant elements","description":"This dataset included the files for both benchmark and the structured segment in the SARS-CoV-2 genome described in the article 'Genome-wide mapping of SARS-CoV-2 RNA structures identifies therapeutically-relevant elements'.  The description of the methods followed is available in the article in detail. Each directory and sub-directory has individual descriptions.\nThe article is available at https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkaa1053 \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-04T18:06:47.731Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8gj97c4kgv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8gj97c4kgv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ilaria","last_name":"Manfredonia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chandran","last_name":"Nithin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Almudena","last_name":"Ponce-Salvatierra","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pritha","last_name":"Ghosh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tomasz K","last_name":"Wirecki","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tycho","last_name":"Marinus","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eric J","last_name":"Snider","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Natacha S","last_name":"Ogando","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martijn J","last_name":" van Hemert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Janusz M","last_name":" Bujnicki","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Danny","last_name":"Incarnato","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r49yg49ddc","title":"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses. Wang et al.","description":"Supplemental Tables S1 to S5 in Microsoft Excel format for the article \"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses\" (Wang et al., 2020):\nTable S1: CRISPR screen results. MaGECK output for positive gene enrichment analysis of SARS-CoV-2, 229E and OC43 host factor screens. Related to Figure 1. \nTable S2: Gene ontology enrichment analysis of individual CRISPR screens and network propagation clusters. Related to Figure 2.\nTable S3: Network propagation results. Related to Figure 2. \nTable S4: RNP editing efficiencies in A549-ACE2 and Huh7.5.1-ACE2/TMPRSS2 cells. Related to Figures 3 and 5.\nTable S5: Oligonucleotide sequences used in this study. Related to STAR Methods. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T18:59:20.104Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r49yg49ddc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r49yg49ddc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Puschnik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":123557,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":116,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":123557,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":123068,"restricted":59,"embargoed":430},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1945},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":625},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":393},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":384},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":381},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":372},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":355},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":309},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":240}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":121020},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1869},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":161},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed",